# --- s009 Book I d.1 q.2 (= Ord. I d.1 p.1 q.2, nn. 23–61)
n("n231","s009-01","Videtur quod sic","la","textual","So A and B (*Videtur quod sic*). Logically the argument is for a plurality of *rationes fruibilitatis*, i.e. ‘no’; Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 1 n. 23: *Et quod sit in eo talis distinctio, probatio*.","verified")
n("n232","s009-01","sicut ens et verum sunt in omni genere","la","textual","B: *sicut ens et vnum sunt in omni genere*. The source is Aristotle, *EN* I.6, 1096a23–29: the good is said in as many ways as being, in all the categories.","verified")
n("n233","s009-01","ibi erit alia ratio fruibilitatis","la","textual","B: *ibi erit alia et alia ratio fruibilitatis*, which is better.","verified")
n("n234","s009-02","In primo articulo secundi","la","textual","B: *In principio articuli secundi*, which gives the right sense (‘at the beginning of the second article’) and is followed in the translation.","verified")
n("n235","s009-02","quadruplici articulo","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 1 n. 30: *Ista quaestio posset habere quadruplicem difficultatem secundum quadruplicem distinctionem in divinis* (essence/person, person/person, essence/attributes, attribute/attribute). Gorris’s four ‘articles’ are a different division: wayfarer; blessed *de potentia Dei absoluta*; blessed *de potentia creaturae*; *de facto*.","verified")
n("n236","s009-02","De secundo articulo dicitur","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 1 n. 34, verbatim: *De secundo articulo dicitur quod non est possibile loquendo de potentia absoluta Dei quod aliquis comprehensor fruatur essentia divina non fruendo persona*.","verified")
n("n237","s009-02","Henrici quodlibeto .2. q. 7.","la","source","Henry of Ghent, *Quodl.* II q. 7 (the number is printed with the *z*-shaped 2) and, for the reply, *Quodl.* I q. 1. Scotus names no one. These attributions are Gorris’s and have not been verified.","to-verify")
n("n238","s009-02","Ad secundum dicendum quod illa que vniformiter respiciunt","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 1 n. 57.","verified")
n("n239","s009-03","Ad quartum dicit quod de facto est vna fruitio","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 1 n. 54: *de facto erit una visio et una fruitio essentiae in tribus personis*, citing Augustine, *De Trin.* I.10.","verified")
n("n240","s009-03","non est in potestate eius vt actus positus","la","commentary","Gorris singles out (*hoc nota diligenter*) Scotus’s principle that the will controls whether an act is posited, but not the modal condition (necessity or freedom) of the act once posited. This is relevant to the later debate on the necessity of beatific enjoyment (*Ord.* I d. 1 p. 2 q. 2).","certain")
n("n241","s009-04","dicitur a quodam","la","source","The view that God and the things known in God are known by one act, with new knowledge of secondary objects arising through new relations, recalls Scotus’s own doctrine of the secondary objects of the beatific act (cf. *Ord.* I d. 1 and *Quodl.* q. 14). Gorris’s *quidam* is not identified.","uncertain")
n("n242","s009-06","Aug. 7. de trini. c. 2°.","la","source","Augustine, *De Trinitate* VII.1.2: *omnis essentia quae relative dicitur est etiam aliquid excepto relativo*. Gorris restricts the rule to first-order relations: a relation founded on a relation (likeness on proportion) leaves something relative when removed.","verified")
n("n243","s009-01","It seems so","en","translation","Both prints have *quod sic*, though the argument proves the contrary of the thesis; see Latin note.")
n("n244","s009-01","being and truth","en","translation","B *unum* (‘one’) for A *verum*.")
n("n245","s009-02","In the first article of the second","en","translation","Rendering A literally. B’s *in principio articuli secundi*, ‘at the beginning of the second article’, is preferable.")
n("n246","s009-02","any one of the blessed","en","translation","*comprehensor*: one who has attained (‘comprehended’) the end, the blessed, as opposed to the *viator*, wayfarer.")
# --- B collation (full): B20R–B21R
upd("n234",text="B: *In principio articuli secundi*, which gives the better sense ('at the beginning of the second article'). A is construable, so the translation renders A (see note n245).")
n("n1038","s009-03","nec ordinate non frui","la","textual","B: *nec ordinate potest non frui*. The translation supplies 'can' in either case.","certain")
upd("n240",append="B: *cum non est in potestate eius* for A's *tamen non est …*.")
n("n1039","s009-04","videt essentiam videt omnes creaturas","la","textual","B: *videt essentiam videt et omnes creaturas*.","certain")
n("n1040","s009-04","habent habitudinem necessariam","la","textual","B: *habent aptitudinem necessariam*.","certain")
n("n1041","s009-05","conuenit beatificari nisi vt in tribus","la","textual","B: *conuenit beatificari non vt in tribus*. B fits the answer better: *sic*, because the essence beatifies under its absolute aspect and not as in three. A is construable, and the translation keeps it.","certain")
n("n1042","s009-07","dico quod est vnus finis","la","textual","B: *dico quod non est vnus finis vltimus in se*. The negation contradicts what follows (*tamen ille habet rationes distinctas*); A is right.","certain")
